  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Solving hidden quadratics using substitutions"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are hidden quadratic equations?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Hidden quadratic equations are quadratics written in terms of a function** **$f(x)$\n- A normal quadratic appears in the form $ax^{2}+bx+c=0$\n- Whereas a hidden quadratic appears in the form `a open square brackets f left parenthesis x right parenthesis close square brackets squared plus b open square brackets f left parenthesis x right parenthesis close square brackets plus c equals 0`\n- This might look complicated but it simply means $x$ has been replaced by $f(x)$\n- e.g. $\\mathrm{sin}^{2}x+2\\mathrm{sin}x-3=0$ is just the hidden quadratic of $x^{2}+2x-3=0$ where $f(x)=\\mathrm{sin}x$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I solve hidden quadratic equations?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- First rearrange the function into the form `a open square brackets f left parenthesis x right parenthesis close square brackets squared plus b open square brackets f left parenthesis x right parenthesis close square brackets plus c equals 0`\n- Replacing the function and solving the 'normal' quadratic first\n- Then substitute the function back into the solutions to solve the original quadratic ![Further Solving of Quadratic Equations Notes Diagram 2, A Level & AS Level Pure Maths Revision Notes](assets/2268b1a94745-2-2-5-further-solving-of-quadratic-equations-not.png)"
  },
